          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Why does acid reflux tend to happen after meals?",
          "answer": "Acid reflux often occurs after meals because certain foods and eating patterns can affect the digestive process.\n\nFatty meals, in particular, can slow down the emptying of the stomach. This means food stays in the stomach for a longer period, extending the time during which reflux can happen. This effect is described in the document \"Eating with reflux: meal size, timing, and finding individual triggers.\"\n\nAdditionally, \"Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD)\" mentions that large or late meals can contribute to reflux.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
